From: Jacob Cook <jacobmcook1984@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Input: axp20x-pek - clear pending PEK interrupts on resume for AXP717

When the power key is the wakeup source, the PEK edge interrupts that
woke the system are still pending in the AXP717 when the IRQ thread runs
after resume, so the press that woke the device is delivered to userspace
and logind suspends the device straight back to sleep.

The driver already handles this for the AXP288 by clearing the pending
POKN bit from the noirq resume callback. Do the same for the AXP717: its
PEK rising/falling edge, long and short press interrupts all live in the
low nibble of IRQ1_STATE, which is write-1-to-clear.

Seen on the Anbernic RG35XX H (H700 + AXP717): every power-key wake from
s2idle was followed by "Power key pressed short" / "Suspending..." from
systemd-logind within the same second.

---
--- a/drivers/input/misc/axp20x-pek.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/axp20x-pek.c
@@ -365,16 +365,24 @@
 {
 	struct axp20x_pek *axp20x_pek = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
-	if (axp20x_pek->axp20x->variant != AXP288_ID)
-		return 0;
-
 	/*
 	 * Clear interrupts from button presses during suspend, to avoid
 	 * a wakeup power-button press getting reported to userspace.
 	 */
-	regmap_write(axp20x_pek->axp20x->regmap,
-		     AXP20X_IRQ1_STATE + AXP288_IRQ_POKN / 8,
-		     BIT(AXP288_IRQ_POKN % 8));
+	switch (axp20x_pek->axp20x->variant) {
+	case AXP288_ID:
+		regmap_write(axp20x_pek->axp20x->regmap,
+			     AXP20X_IRQ1_STATE + AXP288_IRQ_POKN / 8,
+			     BIT(AXP288_IRQ_POKN % 8));
+		break;
+	case AXP717_ID:
+		/* PEK rising/falling edge, long and short press live in IRQ1 */
+		regmap_write(axp20x_pek->axp20x->regmap, AXP717_IRQ1_STATE,
+			     GENMASK(3, 0));
+		break;
+	default:
+		break;
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
